        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 876,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Works out a polynomial at each x you send: given coefficients 1 0 -1 0, the curve is 1 x x x + 0 x x - 1 x + 0, and np.polyval gives its value at each x.\n\nUse it to draw the curve np.polyfit found, to extend a fitted trend into the future, to subtract a trend from data, or simply as a shaping function: a control through a cubic can rise, dip and rise again, and with the right coefficients it can bend any way you like.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.polyval <coefficients: floats ...>\nDefault: no coefficients (send them to the coefficients inlet).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.polyval 1 0 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nx:\nWhere to evaluate: a number, list or array. Every input is evaluated and sent.\n\ncoefficients:\nHighest power first, as np.polyfit gives them, replacing any given as arguments.\nHeld: send them first. A 2-D array (several curves from np.polyfit, one per column) gives one value per curve at each x.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe curve's value at each x.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a ramp from -1 to 1 through x x x - x. The curve starts at 0, rises, dips below zero through the middle and comes back to 0 at 1.\n\nBottom: 2 1 is the straight line 2 x + 1: at 0 1 2 3 it gives 1 3 5 7. A metro re-sends the x values five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyfit finds the coefficients from points. np.interp follows a curve drawn through points. ^ raises to a single power.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 860,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Works out a polynomial at each x you send: given coefficients 1 0 -1 0, the curve is 1 x x x + 0 x x - 1 x + 0, and np.polyval gives its value at each x.\n\nUse it to draw the curve np.polyfit found, to extend a fitted trend into the future, to subtract a trend from data, or simply as a shaping function: a control through a cubic can rise, dip and rise again, and with the right coefficients it can bend any way you like.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.polyval <coefficients: floats ...>\nDefault: no coefficients (send them to the coefficients inlet).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.polyval 1 0 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nx:\nWhere to evaluate: a number, list or array. Every input is evaluated and sent.\n\ncoefficients:\nHighest power first, as np.polyfit gives them, replacing any given as arguments.\nHeld: send them first. A 2-D array (several curves from np.polyfit, one per column) gives one value per curve at each x.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe curve's value at each x.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a ramp from -1 to 1 through x x x - x. The curve starts at 0, rises, dips below zero through the middle and comes back to 0 at 1.\n\nBottom: 2 1 is the straight line 2 x + 1: at 0 1 2 3 it gives 1 3 5 7. A metro re-sends the x values five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyfit finds the coefficients from points. np.interp follows a curve drawn through points. ^ raises to a single power."
        },
